The decrease in volume of the oil under the given pressure can be calculated using the formula for compressibility:

\[
\Delta V = -B \cdot V \cdot \Delta P
\]

Where:
- \( B = 20 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{in}^2/\text{lbf} \) (compressibility)
- \( V = 5 \, \text{ft}^3 \) (initial volume)
- \( \Delta P = 2000 \, \text{lbf/in}^2 \) (change in pressure)

First, ensure all units are consistent. Since \( B \) is given per atmosphere, convert \( \Delta P \) to atmospheres:

\[
1 \, \text{atm} = 14.7 \, \text{lbf/in}^2
\]

\[
\Delta P = \frac{2000}{14.7} \approx 136.05 \, \text{atm}
\]

Now, plug in the values:

\[
\Delta V = -20 \times 10^{-6} \cdot 5 \cdot 136.05 \approx -0.0136 \, \text{ft}^3
\]

Thus, the decrease in volume is approximately \(-0.0136 \, \text{ft}^3\).

**Answer:** (D) -0.0136 ft³
